WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:04.800
It's been more than 20 years since&nbsp;
this hilarious sports comedy came&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:09.760
out. Even though critics of its day didn’t&nbsp;
love it, “The Replacements” has been a movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:14.240
classic for many. Here's what members of&nbsp;
the cast have been up to in the time since.

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:20.640
Beloved veteran actor Keanu Reeves brought&nbsp;
the world's most talented barnacle scraper,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:23.360
quarterback Shane Falco, to&nbsp;
life in "The Replacements."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.920 --> 00:00:28.480
After his turn as the downtrodden&nbsp;
former QB, Keanu Reeves promptly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:34.320
disappeared and was never heard from again.&nbsp;
Oh, wait, that didn't happen. In reality,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:38.960
Reeves has spent the two decades since&nbsp;
"The Replacements" came out becoming one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.960 --> 00:00:44.400
of the biggest movie stars in modern cinema, and&nbsp;
starring in more blockbusters than we can count.

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:49.520
The year after “The Replacements,” he&nbsp;
starred as lovable loser Conor O'Neill&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:57.120
in the heartstring-tugging "Hardball." In 2003,&nbsp;
he reprised his role as Neo for not one but two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.120 --> 00:01:02.480
sequels to "The Matrix," before playing the&nbsp;
title character in 2005's "Constantine."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:08.160
He played undercover cop Bob Arctor&nbsp;
in 2006's drug-fueled, dystopian,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:13.280
rotoscoped Philip K. Dick adaptation "A&nbsp;
Scanner Darkly." Two years later took&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.280 --> 00:01:17.360
on the role of Klaatu in the remake&nbsp;
of "The Day the Earth Stood Still."

00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:22.640
Reeves made his directorial debut in 2013&nbsp;
with "Man of Tai Chi," in which he also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:29.200
played Donaka Mark. A year later, he launched the&nbsp;
massively successful series of "John Wick" movies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:33.440
starring as the titular assassin&nbsp;
in three films so far. "John Wick:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:38.080
Chapter 4" is, at the time of this&nbsp;
video’s release, currently filming.

00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:44.400
Actor Gene Hackman took on the role of demanding&nbsp;
but understanding head coach of the Washington&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:50.560
Sentinels, Jimmy McGinty. Or, as Shane Falco&nbsp;
called him, "that old coach from the '80s.".

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:56.000
A year after "The Replacements," Hackman&nbsp;
appeared in the crime rom-com "Heartbreakers."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.000 --> 00:02:01.600
Alongside Sigourney Weaver, Jennifer Love&nbsp;
Hewitt, and Ray Liotta, he played William,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.600 --> 00:02:06.080
the unwitting mark of a marriage&nbsp;
con. In 2001 he starred as Royal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.080 --> 00:02:12.160
the father of the eccentric titular family in Wes&nbsp;
Anderson's quirky comedy "The Royal Tenenbaums,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.160 --> 00:02:16.800
co-starring Ben Stiller, Gwyneth&nbsp;
Paltrow, Owen Wilson, and Luke Wilson.

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:21.520
The same year, Hackman teamed with Owen&nbsp;
Wilson again for the war thriller "Behind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.520 --> 00:02:26.960
Enemy Lines," playing Navy admiral Leslie&nbsp;
Reigart. He was the commanding officer of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:32.240
the USS Carl Vinson whose fighter pilot was&nbsp;
shot down on a routine reconnaissance mission.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.960 --> 00:02:36.160
In 2003, he starred alongside Dustin Hoffman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.160 --> 00:02:42.000
Rachel Weisz, and John Cusack in the legal drama&nbsp;
"Runaway Jury," adapting a John Grisham novel.

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:48.880
In his final film role before retiring, Hackman&nbsp;
starred in the 2004 political comedy "Welcome to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.880 --> 00:02:54.800
Mooseport" with Ray Romano. He played former&nbsp;
United States president Monroe "Eagle" Cole,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.800 --> 00:03:00.080
who gets drawn into an increasingly silly&nbsp;
campaign to become mayor of a small town in Maine.

00:03:01.280 --> 00:03:05.280
Head cheerleader Annabelle Farrell was&nbsp;
brought to life by actor Brooke Langton.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.840 --> 00:03:12.080
Not only is she an amazing on-field performer,&nbsp;
she's a huge football fan. Even if Shane’s entire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.080 --> 00:03:16.320
team turned against him, he would always&nbsp;
have one true believer on the sidelines.

00:03:16.960 --> 00:03:20.560
Two years after appearing in "The&nbsp;
Replacements," Langton showed up in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.560 --> 00:03:27.520
many guest roles on popular TV shows like&nbsp;
"Weeds" in 2005 and "Monk" in 2006. Langton&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.520 --> 00:03:34.080
reunited with "The Replacements" co-star Orlando&nbsp;
Jones for the B-horror movie "Primeval" in 2007,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:39.920
playing a journalist who travels to Burundi to&nbsp;
try to capture a giant, man-eating crocodile.

00:03:39.920 --> 00:03:45.280
Later that year, she joined the main cast&nbsp;
of NBC's crime drama "Life," playing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.280 --> 00:03:49.680
role of Constance Griffiths. She’s the&nbsp;
attorney who proved the innocence of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:54.560
wrongly-convicted lead character Detective&nbsp;
Charlie Crews, played by Damian Lewis.

00:03:55.200 --> 00:03:59.840
In the years since, Langton has appeared&nbsp;
in a few other popular series and films,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.840 --> 00:04:06.160
like “The Debt” in 2015. In 2018, she took on the&nbsp;
recurring role of Lieutenant Maddie Rawlings on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.160 --> 00:04:11.920
TNT's action-drama series "The Last Ship" for&nbsp;
five episodes, working alongside the rest of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.920 --> 00:04:17.360
the crew of the USS Nathan James to try to find&nbsp;
a cure for the pandemic that ravages the planet.

00:04:18.400 --> 00:04:23.760
Actor Orlando Jones was a perfect choice&nbsp;
for the role of less-than-sure-handed wide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:29.600
receiver Clifford Franklin. He might have had&nbsp;
some serious wheels and major breakaway speed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:34.880
but he sure didn't have the mitts and dexterity&nbsp;
required to haul in a pass with any consistency.

00:04:35.520 --> 00:04:40.240
Jones kept plenty busy following his turn as&nbsp;
Franklin. A few months after "The Replacements"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.240 --> 00:04:46.240
was released, he took on multiple roles in the&nbsp;
Faustian comedy remake of "Bedazzled." There,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.240 --> 00:04:51.440
Elizabeth Hurley plays the Devil, enticing Brendan&nbsp;
Frazer's hapless character to sell his soul.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:56.080
The next year, Jones played community&nbsp;
college professor Harry Block&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.080 --> 00:05:01.760
in the underrated sci-fi comedy "Evolution,"&nbsp;
fighting an alien invasion. A year later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.760 --> 00:05:07.200
he appeared as the digital artificial&nbsp;
intelligence librarian Vox 114 in "The Time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:13.440
Machine," a loose adaptation of the classic&nbsp;
H.G. Wells novel of the same name. In 2003,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.440 --> 00:05:17.680
Jones joined Gene Hackman as part&nbsp;
of the cast of "Runaway Jury."

00:05:17.680 --> 00:05:23.440
In addition to his work in feature films, Jones&nbsp;
has enjoyed plenty of success on the small screen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.440 --> 00:05:28.080
including his turn as Captain Frank&nbsp;
Irving on the supernatural procedural&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.080 --> 00:05:35.680
"Sleepy Hollow." He also nabbed a recurring&nbsp;
role as Mr. Nancy, aka the tricker god Anansi,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.680 --> 00:05:38.800
on the popular Neil Gaiman series "American Gods."

00:05:40.400 --> 00:05:43.680
Kicker Nigel Gruff, as played by actor Rhys Ifans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.680 --> 00:05:49.120
isn't exactly what you'd call athletic — though&nbsp;
it's clear his liver gets a regular workout.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.120 --> 00:05:53.040
The former soccer striker is&nbsp;
more what you'd call "wiry."

00:05:53.040 --> 00:05:56.800
Three months after the release of&nbsp;
"The Replacements," Ifans appeared&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.800 --> 00:06:02.640
alongside another talented cast, taking on&nbsp;
the role of Adrian, one of the sons of Satan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.640 --> 00:06:07.840
in Adam Sandler's "Little Nicky."&nbsp;
In 2001, he took on the role of Iki,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.840 --> 00:06:14.640
a club owner who also deals in drugs and guns&nbsp;
in "The 51st State." The film starred Samuel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.640 --> 00:06:20.960
L. Jackson as a revolutionary drug chemist&nbsp;
and was alternately titled "Formula 51."

00:06:20.960 --> 00:06:27.440
In 2010, Ifans had an iconic turn as Xenophilius&nbsp;
Lovegood, publisher of the Quibbler and father&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.440 --> 00:06:34.000
to Luna Lovegood in "Harry Potter and the Deathly&nbsp;
Hallows: Part 1." Two years later, he played Dr.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.000 --> 00:06:39.840
Curt Connors, mentor to Peter Parker, played by&nbsp;
Andrew Garfield, and eventual reptilian villain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.840 --> 00:06:46.640
in "The Amazing Spider-Man." Ifans also enjoyed&nbsp;
a recurring role as Mycroft Holmes, brother to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.640 --> 00:06:52.560
Sherlock Holmes, played by Jonny Lee Miller, on&nbsp;
the modern American crime procedural "Elementary."

00:06:53.280 --> 00:06:58.720
Actor Michael Jace portrayed defensive back&nbsp;
Earl Wilkinson, who went to prison for allegedly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.720 --> 00:07:05.600
beating up a cop — naturally he mixes well with&nbsp;
Danny Bateman. As such, Wilkinson wears a tinted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.600 --> 00:07:12.000
visor on his helmet and plays under the alias Ray&nbsp;
Smith. This is so no one’s aware that a convicted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:16.480
felon has been released by the governor to&nbsp;
serve as a replacement player during the strike.

00:07:17.120 --> 00:07:22.880
Jace is perhaps best known as Julien Lowe,&nbsp;
a uniformed police officer and member of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.880 --> 00:07:29.680
main cast of "The Shield," FX's gritty crime&nbsp;
drama. He appeared in all 88 of the series'&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.680 --> 00:07:36.720
episodes across its seven seasons, portraying a&nbsp;
cop enduring the inner conflict his homosexuality&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.720 --> 00:07:41.600
brought about with his devout Christian&nbsp;
faith. Lead character Vic Mackey is aware&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.600 --> 00:07:45.680
of Julien's sexual orientation and&nbsp;
blackmails him to keep a secret.

00:07:46.240 --> 00:07:53.360
Jace's final credited role was recurring character&nbsp;
Terrell on the crime drama "Southland." In 2014,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.360 --> 00:07:59.440
according to CNN, Jace was arrested and charged&nbsp;
with homicide in the fatal shooting of his wife,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.440 --> 00:08:07.040
April Jace, having reportedly dialed 911 himself.&nbsp;
Michael Jace was convicted of second-degree murder&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.040 --> 00:08:14.080
in May 2016, per ABC7, which reported the jury&nbsp;
deliberated for fewer than three hours before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.080 --> 00:08:20.480
reaching a guilty verdict. A month later, Jace&nbsp;
was sentenced to 40 years to life in prison.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.480 --> 00:08:27.040
He is currently incarcerated in California State&nbsp;
Prison and will be eligible for parole in 2039&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.040 --> 00:08:30.480
according to the California Department&nbsp;
of Corrections and Rehabilitation.

00:08:31.840 --> 00:08:36.000
If there's one thing for which&nbsp;
quarterback Shane Falco can be grateful,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.000 --> 00:08:41.120
it's that linebacker Danny Bateman,&nbsp;
played by Jon Favreau, is on his team.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.120 --> 00:08:46.080
This means that he's only on the receiving end&nbsp;
of his crushing tackles when Bateman forgets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.080 --> 00:08:51.360
that he's not supposed to hit players wearing&nbsp;
red jerseys in practice. Opposing QBs had better&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.360 --> 00:08:57.200
look out, because he hits hard and fast, despite&nbsp;
having only played college football as a walk-on.

00:08:57.200 --> 00:09:02.720
The year after "The Replacements" came out,&nbsp;
Favreau wrote and directed his first feature film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.720 --> 00:09:08.400
"Made," in which he also starred. He appeared&nbsp;
alongside Vince Vaughn as a mediocre boxer who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.400 --> 00:09:14.560
gets involved in low-level organized crime. Two&nbsp;
years later, Favreau took on the role of Franklin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.560 --> 00:09:19.360
"Foggy" Nelson in the much-maligned,&nbsp;
Ben Affleck-starring Marvel Comics movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.360 --> 00:09:25.040
"Daredevil." Of course, in 2008, he helped&nbsp;
launch the Marvel Cinematic Universe as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.040 --> 00:09:30.240
Harold "Happy" Hogan, Tony Stark's driver,&nbsp;
bodyguard and best friend in "Iron Man."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.800 --> 00:09:36.640
Favreau appeared in all three "Iron Man" solo&nbsp;
films — as well as directing the first two — in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.640 --> 00:09:42.800
addition to helping to mentor Peter Parker, Tom&nbsp;
Holland, in the MCU's two "Spider-Man" movies.

00:09:42.800 --> 00:09:48.480
Favreau has continued to direct and produce,&nbsp;
helming the live-action remakes of Disney's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.480 --> 00:09:54.080
"The Jungle Book'' and "The Lion King." If all&nbsp;
of that wasn't enough, he is also the creator&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.080 --> 00:09:59.440
and serves as showrunner of "The Mandalorian,"&nbsp;
the "Star Wars" streaming series on Disney+.

00:10:00.800 --> 00:10:06.160
According to Coach Jimmy McGinty, tight&nbsp;
end Brian Murphy, played by David Denman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.160 --> 00:10:11.520
would have been drafted in the first round if&nbsp;
he hadn't been born deaf. Despite living with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.520 --> 00:10:17.520
such a limitation, Brian manages to excel as a&nbsp;
member of the replacement Washington Sentinels.

00:10:17.520 --> 00:10:20.720
“Look at it this way: he’ll never&nbsp;
be called offsides on an audible.”

00:10:21.840 --> 00:10:25.760
Denman’s turn as Brian was his first&nbsp;
appearance in a feature-length film.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.760 --> 00:10:32.160
In 2001, he took on the role of Lance, the gay&nbsp;
bartender at the fictional Alaskan ski resort&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.160 --> 00:10:38.080
of Bull Mountain in the snowboarding comedy&nbsp;
"Out Cold." Denman played bully Don Price,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.080 --> 00:10:43.520
who beats up protagonist Edward Bloom&nbsp;
in Tim Burton's 2003 fantasy film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.520 --> 00:10:48.880
"Big Fish." Denman's most recent film&nbsp;
role came in 2020's "Greenland," for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.880 --> 00:10:54.240
which he took on the character Ralph Vento,&nbsp;
who kidnaps John Garrity's son Nathan.

00:10:54.960 --> 00:11:00.240
Denman has also enjoyed memorable small screen&nbsp;
roles, including appearing as Ed Brooks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.240 --> 00:11:05.120
the adulterous love interest of Julia&nbsp;
Braverman-Graham. That was on the NBC&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.120 --> 00:11:10.640
family dramedy series "Parenthood." He also&nbsp;
recently took on the role of Frank Sheehan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.640 --> 00:11:15.720
ex-husband to the title character in the&nbsp;
acclaimed HBO limited series "Mare of Easttown."

00:11:16.120 --> 00:11:23.040
Ace Yonamine took on the larger-than-life role&nbsp;
of "Jumbo" Fumiko, a member of the replacement&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.040 --> 00:11:28.720
Washington Sentinels' offensive line. He's a&nbsp;
former sumo wrestler, which makes him an expert&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.720 --> 00:11:34.640
at pushing people around. As Coach Jimmy McGinty&nbsp;
says, in the world of professional football,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.640 --> 00:11:40.880
that translates to pass blocking. Fumiko's also&nbsp;
the big hero of the bar fight, having sat on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.880 --> 00:11:47.040
Wilson's head, and his kiai becomes a popular&nbsp;
rallying cry among the team. Unfortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.040 --> 00:11:52.720
he commits a holding penalty when Shane Falco&nbsp;
improbably runs the ball in for a touchdown on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.720 --> 00:11:58.080
the field goal attempt, during which Nigel Gruff&nbsp;
was nearly forced to blow the kick on purpose.

00:11:58.640 --> 00:12:04.080
Yonamine's role in "The Replacements" is an&nbsp;
instance of art imitating life; prior to his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:04.080 --> 00:12:09.280
appearance in the movie — his feature film debut&nbsp;
— the Hawaiian born actor worked in Japan as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:09.280 --> 00:12:14.560
sumo wrestler, according to Hawaiian outlet&nbsp;
Midweek. To date, Yonamine has only appeared&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.560 --> 00:12:21.280
in two additional films, according to IMDb. In&nbsp;
2003, he appeared in "Lara Croft Tomb Raider:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.280 --> 00:12:27.120
The Cradle of Life." He was an imposing member&nbsp;
of the criminal organization Shay Ling, which was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:27.120 --> 00:12:34.000
hired by Dr. Jonathan Reiss to retrieve the map&nbsp;
to the mystical object. In 2005, Yonamine appeared&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.000 --> 00:12:39.600
as a sumo wrestler in "Memoirs of a Geisha."&nbsp;
According to Midweek, he's continued acting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:39.600 --> 00:12:45.200
by appearing in commercials and works security&nbsp;
at the courthouse in Pearl City, Oahu, Hawaii.

00:12:46.160 --> 00:12:52.320
Jamal Jackson, as played by actor Faizon Love,&nbsp;
is a bit of a hothead. He and brother Andre are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:52.320 --> 00:12:57.200
a formidable pair of guards that Coach Jimmy&nbsp;
McGinty recruits back into football after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:57.200 --> 00:13:02.320
they retire and become actual bodyguards.&nbsp;
After the bar fight that unites the team,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:02.320 --> 00:13:08.240
the brothers realize it's their job to protect&nbsp;
quarterback Shane Falco on and off the field,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:08.240 --> 00:13:13.360
so when the striking players harass quarterback&nbsp;
Shane Falco in the parking lot a second time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:13.360 --> 00:13:17.840
Jamal pulls a gun and shoots&nbsp;
Eddie Martel's car multiple times.

00:13:18.400 --> 00:13:21.120
A year after appearing in "The Replacements,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:21.120 --> 00:13:26.240
Love showed up in various films, including&nbsp;
“Couples Retreat” with co-star Jon Favreau,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:26.240 --> 00:13:32.080
who also co-wrote the film. He took on the role&nbsp;
of Shane, the recently divorced guy with a wild,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:32.080 --> 00:13:38.240
20-something girlfriend. In 2003, he appeared&nbsp;
in the charming Will Ferrell Christmas comedy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:38.240 --> 00:13:44.000
"Elf" — also directed by Favreau — as Wanda. He’s&nbsp;
the manager of the Gimbels department store who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:44.000 --> 00:13:49.440
sends Buddy, played by Ferrell, into a frenzy&nbsp;
by announcing that Santa Claus would be coming.

00:13:49.440 --> 00:13:54.640
In addition to finding steady work as a&nbsp;
character actor in films, Love also appeared&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:54.640 --> 00:14:01.120
in nine episodes of Kevin Hart's BET reality&nbsp;
TV parody "Real Husbands of Hollywood." He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:01.120 --> 00:14:07.440
played a fictionalized version of himself in the&nbsp;
show's first, fifth, and sixth seasons. In 2018,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:07.440 --> 00:14:13.120
Love joined the cast of "Step Up: High Water"&nbsp;
— a drama series based on the popular "Step&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:13.120 --> 00:14:18.720
Up" movies — as Al Baker. Love appeared in the&nbsp;
first two seasons that aired on YouTube Red.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:18.720 --> 00:14:23.120
"Step Up" was picked up for a third&nbsp;
season by premium cable network Starz.

00:14:24.480 --> 00:14:30.880
Actor Michael Taliferro took on the role of Andre&nbsp;
Jackson, the larger brother to Jamal who is a bit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:30.880 --> 00:14:35.760
more level-headed, though just as protective&nbsp;
of Shane Falco once the team comes together.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:35.760 --> 00:14:40.400
In that memorable parking lot scene, Andre&nbsp;
reveals that he's pretty fond of the smell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:40.400 --> 00:14:45.520
of that wild yam that Annabelle Farrell&nbsp;
gave Shane to soothe his sore muscles.

00:14:46.240 --> 00:14:47.040
“What’s that smell?”

00:14:48.000 --> 00:14:48.640
“Wild yam.”

00:14:48.640 --> 00:14:50.160
“Mmm. That’s nice.”

00:14:51.040 --> 00:14:55.920
Following "The Replacements," Taliferro's&nbsp;
next film appearance came in the independent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:55.920 --> 00:15:02.240
crime drama "Blue Hill Avenue." He took on the&nbsp;
role of Simon, an adrenaline-fueled criminal who,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:02.240 --> 00:15:07.440
with his three friends, is one of the&nbsp;
biggest drug dealers in Boston. In 2002,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:07.440 --> 00:15:12.880
Taliferro appeared as inmate "Little Joe" in the&nbsp;
crime thriller "Half Past Dead," helping Steven&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:12.880 --> 00:15:19.840
Seagal's FBI agent character rescue a kidnapped&nbsp;
Supreme Court justice on Alcatraz. For 2004's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:19.840 --> 00:15:25.280
dance film "You Got Served," he played Emerald.&nbsp;
He was the drug lord for whom lead characters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:25.280 --> 00:15:30.960
Elgin and David work in order to pay for the Lil&nbsp;
Saints to enter their street dancing battles.

00:15:30.960 --> 00:15:36.720
Taliferro's final film role came as Brian Smith&nbsp;
in the crime musical "A Day in the Life," a film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:36.720 --> 00:15:42.880
that was released in 2009, a little more&nbsp;
than three years after his death in 2006.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:42.880 --> 00:15:46.480
As the Tampa Bay Times reported, he died on May 4&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:46.480 --> 00:15:51.680
at the age of 44 after suffering a stroke&nbsp;
near his home in Los Angeles, California.

00:15:52.720 --> 00:15:59.040
Troy Winbush brought to life pious running back&nbsp;
Walter Cochran. Cochran is one of the replacement&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:59.040 --> 00:16:04.720
Washington Sentinels with some actual experience&nbsp;
playing professional football, though not much. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:04.720 --> 00:16:10.560
played one game in the pros and blew out his knee,&nbsp;
pretty much ending his career — or so he thought.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:10.560 --> 00:16:16.160
When given a second chance at playing football, he&nbsp;
just wants to score one touchdown. Thankfully he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:16.160 --> 00:16:22.400
manages to do just that, though he blows out his&nbsp;
knee again in the process. Cochran notably makes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:22.400 --> 00:16:27.280
Shane Falco promise to finish what he started.&nbsp;
We assume he’s telling him to win the game.

00:16:28.080 --> 00:16:32.160
One of Winbush's next feature film&nbsp;
roles came in the 2002 thriller&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:32.160 --> 00:16:36.240
"John Q," in which he appeared&nbsp;
as expectant father Steve Smith.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:36.240 --> 00:16:41.760
He is one of the character John Archibald's&nbsp;
hostages. Denzel Washington’s role is of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:41.760 --> 00:16:46.320
desperate father who seeks to get his son on&nbsp;
the recipient list for a heart transplant.

00:16:46.880 --> 00:16:51.200
In the years since, Winbush has found&nbsp;
consistent work as an actor taking on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:51.200 --> 00:16:56.160
guest roles in various popular TV series.&nbsp;
He's appeared in shows ranging from police&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:56.160 --> 00:17:01.920
procedurals like “NCIS” to dramas like&nbsp;
"Saving Grace.” He's also had a recurring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:01.920 --> 00:17:07.920
role as Officer Puchinski on the popular sitcom&nbsp;
"The Goldbergs." Winbush recently appeared in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:07.920 --> 00:17:14.320
Season 1 of "The Wilds" — the streaming drama&nbsp;
from Amazon Studios — as FBI Agent Dean Young.

00:17:15.520 --> 00:17:21.280
Ugh, Eddie Martel, played by Brett&nbsp;
Cullen. Where to even start with this guy?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:21.280 --> 00:17:25.440
He's a prima donna, ending the final&nbsp;
game before the player's strike with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:25.440 --> 00:17:31.360
a giveaway slide instead of going for the win.&nbsp;
Martel's also an unrepentant jerk who has his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:31.360 --> 00:17:36.400
fellow striking players flip Shane Falco's&nbsp;
already-beat up truck in the parking lot.

00:17:36.400 --> 00:17:41.760
In reality, we hope Cullen is a decent person&nbsp;
and we've got no reason to believe otherwise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:41.760 --> 00:17:44.800
though he's played some other sketchy&nbsp;
characters in the years since.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:45.360 --> 00:17:50.080
Starting in 2005, he took on the&nbsp;
recurring role of Goodwin Stanhope,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:50.080 --> 00:17:55.760
one of the Others sent to infiltrate the group&nbsp;
of surviving passengers of Oceanic Flight 815&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:55.760 --> 00:18:01.760
on "Lost." Cullen also appeared as the&nbsp;
creepy, adulterous congressman in 2012's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:01.760 --> 00:18:07.440
"The Dark Knight Rises," though that wouldn't&nbsp;
be his last role in a DC Comics Batman film.

00:18:07.440 --> 00:18:10.720
Having seemingly perfected&nbsp;
playing an entitled jerk,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:10.720 --> 00:18:16.400
he took on the role of Thomas Wayne, the father&nbsp;
of the future Caped Crusader Bruce Wayne who's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:16.400 --> 00:18:23.360
also maybe-maybe-not the father of Arthur Fleck,&nbsp;
played by Joaquin Phoenix. He is, of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:23.360 --> 00:18:29.040
the Joker, in 2019 the 2019 film, "Joker."&nbsp;
Cullen also had a recurring role on "The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:29.040 --> 00:18:34.560
Blacklist" beginning in the finale episode&nbsp;
of Season 6. He also appeared in Season 3&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:34.560 --> 00:18:39.840
of "True Detective" as the eventual&nbsp;
Arkansas Attorney General Gerald Kindt.

00:18:40.960 --> 00:18:46.240
Veteran actor Jack Warden brought Washington&nbsp;
Sentinels owner Edward O'Neil to life.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:46.240 --> 00:18:51.600
He's an old-school kind of guy and he hilariously&nbsp;
yells at Eddie Martel from his luxury box.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:52.160 --> 00:18:56.880
He declares the cocky quarterback to be a&nbsp;
wimp, after his give-up slide at the end&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:56.880 --> 00:19:00.800
of the final pre-strike game. Though&nbsp;
he uses a different word altogether&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:00.800 --> 00:19:05.840
when courting Jimmy McGinty to be his&nbsp;
new head coach. In said negotiations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:05.840 --> 00:19:11.840
he promises to give Coach McGinty full control of&nbsp;
the team. Spoiler alert, he breaks that promise.

00:19:12.560 --> 00:19:15.600
After Martel tells McGinty that O'Neil will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:15.600 --> 00:19:20.000
fire him when Shane Falco shows up&nbsp;
during halftime of the final game,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:20.000 --> 00:19:24.960
the furious coach yells something that elaborates&nbsp;
on the troubled history between the two.

00:19:24.960 --> 00:19:26.240
"It won't be the first time.”

00:19:27.040 --> 00:19:32.400
"The Replacements" marked Warden's final&nbsp;
film appearance, as he retired from acting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:32.400 --> 00:19:37.680
citing concerns of declining health. He&nbsp;
turned 80 the year the film came out. As&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:37.680 --> 00:19:45.040
the BBC reported in 2006, Warden died at age&nbsp;
85 after weeks of poor health. His manager had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:45.040 --> 00:19:49.440
told the outlet that Warden had suffered from&nbsp;
heart and kidney problems prior to his death.

